-- title:
--   Puder, Claim 3.3(4) — if Γ_X(H) is a subgraph of Γ_X(J), then H is a free factor of J
-- statement:
--   Let $H \le J$ be subgroups of $\mathbf F_d$. Suppose that every element $g \in J$ whose reduced walk from the basepoint of the Schreier coset graph of $J$ crosses only edges that are also crossed by the walk of some element of $H$ (`Puder.Traverses`) lies in $H$. Then $H$ is a free factor of $J$ (`Puder.IsFreeFactor`): $H$ is generated by a subset of some basis of $J$.
--
--   Puder, p. 14: “**Claim 3.3.** *Let $H, J, L \le \mathbf F_k$ be subgroups. Then* […] *(4) If $\Gamma_X(H)$ is a subgraph of $\Gamma_X(J)$, namely if $\eta^X_{H\to J}$ is injective, then $H \le_{ff} J$.*”
--
--   *Formalization note.* The morphism $\eta^X_{H\to J}$ is not defined in the bundles; the hypothesis is stated through its image. The edges of $\Gamma_X(J)$ crossed by walks of elements of $H$ form the image $\Delta = \operatorname{im}\eta^X_{H\to J}$, and the hypothesis says that every element of $J$ whose walk stays in $\Delta$ lies in $H$, i.e. $H = \pi_1^X(\Delta)$ (the inclusion $H \subseteq \pi_1^X(\Delta)$ always holds). This is what injectivity of $\eta^X_{H\to J}$ gives, and it is the form in which Puder applies the claim in the proof of Lemma 4.1, to $J = \pi^X_1(\Delta)$ for the subgraph $\Delta$ traced by $w$. The statement holds for all subgroups, not only finitely generated ones, as in the source.
--
--   Source: D. Puder, *Expansion of random graphs: new proofs, new results*, Invent. Math. 201 (2015) 845–908, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-014-0560-x (arXiv:1212.5216, p. 14), Claim 3.3(4), which refers to Stallings (1983), Kapovich–Myasnikov (2002), Miasnikov–Ventura–Weil (2007) and Puder (2014) for proofs.
-- source:
--   Puder, D., Expansion of random graphs: new proofs, new results, Invent. Math. 201 (2015) 845–908, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-014-0560-x (arXiv:1212.5216), p. 14, Claim 3.3(4), which cites Stallings (1983), Kapovich–Myasnikov (2002), Miasnikov–Ventura–Weil (2007) and Puder, D., Primitive words, free factors and measure preservation, Israel J. Math. 201 (2014) 25–73, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-013-0055-2 (arXiv:1104.3991)

